Description: scene: Keep a stereo surface's damage on the outputs it is on
 A full side by side surface's buffer holds both eyes, each covering the whole
 surface, but SurfaceItem::addDamage took the whole buffer for the scale of a
 view. The buffer is twice the surface wide, so the scale was 2 against the
 output's 1 and the damage grew by a pixel on every side, into the output next
 to a full screen stereo window. That output then rendered a frame for every
 picture of the film while nothing on it changed: about 24 frames a second of
 1080p30 frame packing on the A10 (ORDER 224, bug 2), doubling the work of a
 weak GPU. The scale of a view is now the eye's pixels to the surface.
 .
 testStereoOutputRepaint puts a 3D film full screen on one output and counts
 the frames another output asks for: it failed with 25 of 25 pictures before
 the change (for a 2D, a frame packing and an anaglyph second output) and
 passes after; the same film in a window and a 2D film full screen are the
 controls.
